This is not harmless. `resolveYamlInteger$1`'s *explicit* pattern (`!!int` tag) accepts signed radix forms like `-0x1F`, `+0x1F`, `-0b101`, `-0o17`. Previously, the sign was peeled off before dispatching on the `0b`/`0o`/`0x` prefix. Now the sign stays in the string, the prefix checks (`startsWith("0x")` etc.) all miss, and `parseInt("-0x1F", 10)` is computed, yielding `-0` (i.e. 0) instead of `-31`. Plain decimals like `-5` still work because `parseInt` itself handles the sign, and implicit resolution (the `^-?(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*)$` pattern) never contains `+` or radix prefixes — which is exactly why the library's own suite stays green. The core (`parseYamlInteger$2`) and YAML 1.1 parsers are untouched, so only explicitly tagged integers under `JSON_SCHEMA` expose the regression.

